  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will cross the eastern Great Lakes \nWednesday through Wednesday night, producing showers and scattered \nthunderstorms. A few storms may be severe with isolated damaging \nwind gusts the primary hazard.\n\nIncreasing warm advection and moisture transport ahead of the next \nlow pressure system will bring a west to east increase in high \nclouds this afternoon, lowering into a mid-level cloud deck tonight. \nDry weather will prevail through daybreak Wednesday. \n\nOngoing warm advection and isentropic upglide will support an area \nof showers across the North Country from midday into the afternoon. \nA few scattered light showers may also clip Western NY, mainly along \nand North of the NYS Thruway Wednesday morning through mid \nafternoon. \n\nThe main area of showers and scattered thunderstorms will arrive \nlate Wednesday afternoon through late evening as a broken band of \nshowers and scattered thunderstorms cross the region. A sharp mid \nlevel trough will cross Ontario and Quebec, with the southern extent \nof height falls spreading east across the Great Lakes and providing \nsome large scale forcing for ascent. The main low level focus for \nconvection will be a pre-frontal trough that is forecast to reach \nthe eastern Great Lakes by early evening. The system will capture a \nnarrow plume of deep moisture, with PWAT values briefly exceeding 2 \ninches late Wednesday just ahead of the pre-frontal trough.\n\nAny thunderstorms will produce brief, torrential downpours, but fast \nstorm motion will likely keep the flood risk low. Strong shear will \noverspread the area Wednesday, with boundary layer flow increasing \nto 40+ knots. Poor mid level lapse rates will keep instability \nsomewhat limited, but the latest guidance suggests that sufficient \ninstability may develop to support some severe risk from late \nafternoon through mid to late evening. The largely unidirectional \nwind profiles will support localized damaging wind gusts as the \nprimary hazard.\n\nShowers and scattered thunderstorms will taper off from northwest to \nsoutheast overnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few showers may linger Thursday mainly across the \nSouthern Tier, with dry weather returning Friday through Saturday.\n\nThe cold front will stall across Ohio and PA Thursday as a weak \nbaroclinic wave moves east down the boundary. This wave will produce \nan area of organized rain to our south across Ohio and PA. Most of \nthis will miss our region, although a few showers may develop across \nthe Southern Tier. Otherwise, the rest of the area will be mainly \ndry Thursday with a good deal of cloud cover north of the frontal \nwave. The front will then accelerate southeast and into the Mid \nAtlantic Thursday night as high pressure builds into the central \nGreat Lakes.\n\nDry weather and sunshine will return Friday through Saturday as high \npressure drifts from the Great Lakes east to New England. The next \nchance of showers may arrive by Sunday as the next mid level trough \nand cold front move into the Great Lakes.",
